{#ch21-0001}
# The First Lay of Guðrún

{#ch21-0002 ref="gdr1.1"}
::: stanza
Early it was,[^6] when Guðrún got herself ready to die,[^7]
when she sat sorrowful over Sigurðr;[^8]
she did not howl or strike with her hands,[^9]
or wail about it like other women.[^10]
:::

{#ch21-0003 ref="gdr1.2"}
::: stanza
Very wise earls[^11] came forward,
they who tried to dissuade her from hardness of heart;
yet Guðrún could not cry at all —
so impassioned was she, she might have burst apart.
:::

{#ch21-0004 ref="gdr1.3"}
::: stanza
Stately brides of earls[^12] sat,
adorned with gold, before Guðrún;
each of them spoke of her[^13] extreme sorrow,
the severest that she had suffered.
:::

{#ch21-0005 ref="gdr1.4"}
::: stanza
Then Gjaflaug [^14] spoke, Gjúki’s sister:
‘I know myself \[to be\] the most bereft of love on earth:
I have experienced the loss of five husbands,
two daughters, three sisters,
eight brothers — yet I live \[on\], alone!’
:::

{#ch21-0006 ref="gdr1.5"}
::: stanza
Yet Guðrún could not cry at all:
so impassioned was she at the young man’s death
and hard-hearted over the marshal’s corpse.
:::

{#ch21-0007 ref="gdr1.6"}
::: stanza
Then Herborg [^15] said this, queen of Húnaland:[^16]
‘I have a harsher harm to speak of:
my seven sons, in a southern land,
\[and my\] husband the eighth, fell in battle;
:::

{#ch21-0008 ref="gdr1.7"}
::: stanza
‘\[my\] father and mother, \[and\] four brothers,
the wind played with them on the wave,
a billow struck the board-wall.[^17]
:::

{#ch21-0009 ref="gdr1.8"}
::: stanza
‘I myself had to adorn them,[^18] I myself had to bury them,
I myself had to handle their Hel-journey;[^19]
all that I experienced in one season,[^20]
so that no one made me happy.
:::

{#ch21-0010 ref="gdr1.9"}
::: stanza
‘Then I was compelled to become a captive and a prisoner of war
in the same season thereafter;
I had to adorn[^21] and to bind the shoes of
a chieftain’s wife each morning.
:::

{#ch21-0011 ref="gdr1.10"}
::: stanza
‘She intimidated me out of envy,
and hit me with hard blows;
I found a better house-man nowhere,
but a worse house-lady nowhere!’
:::

{#ch21-0012 ref="gdr1.11"}
::: stanza
Yet Guðrún could not cry at all:
so impassioned was she at the young man’s death
and hard-hearted over the marshal’s corpse.
:::

{#ch21-0013 ref="gdr1.12"}
::: stanza
Then Gullrǫnd [^22] said this, Gjúki’s daughter:
‘Little do you know, foster-mother,[^23] even though you are wise,
how to broach conversation with a young wife!’
She advised against covering the marshal’s corpse.
:::

{#ch21-0014 ref="gdr1.13"}
::: stanza
She swept the shroud from Sigurðr
and placed a pillow[^24] before the wife’s knees:
‘Look at your loved one, lay your mouth against his moustache,
as you used to embrace the leader while he was whole!’
:::

{#ch21-0015 ref="gdr1.14"}
::: stanza
Guðrún looked at him one time \[only\];
she saw Dagr’s descendant’s[^25] hair streaming with blood,
the sparkling eyes of the marshal \[that had\] passed away,[^26]
the boar’s[^27] heart-stronghold[^28] cut open by a sword.
:::

{#ch21-0016 ref="gdr1.15"}
::: stanza
Then Guðrún sank, leant against the bolster;
her hair came loose, her cheek reddened,
and a drop of rain[^29] ran down over her knees.
:::

{#ch21-0017 ref="gdr1.16"}
::: stanza
Then Guðrún wept, Gjúki’s daughter,
so that tears flew through her tresses,[^30]
and at that geese honked[^31] in the home-meadow,[^32]
excellent birds which the young woman owned.
:::

{#ch21-0018 ref="gdr1.17"}
::: stanza
Then Gullrǫnd said this, Gjúki’s daughter:[^33]
‘I know your love[^34] was the greatest
of all people upon earth;
you were happy nowhere, \[neither\] outside nor inside,
my sister, unless beside Sigurðr!’
:::

{#ch21-0019 ref="gdr1.18"}
::: stanza
[^35] ‘So was my Sigurðr beside the sons of Gjúki,
as if he were a spear-leek[^36] grown higher than the grass,
or a bright stone[^37] strung on a band,[^38]
a noble stone over noblemen\!
:::

{#ch21-0020 ref="gdr1.19"}
::: stanza
‘I also seemed to the king’s men
higher than any woman of Herjann;[^39]
now I am as little as a leaf often is
on(?) bay willows,[^40] after the dead boar\![^41]
:::

{#ch21-0021 ref="gdr1.20"}
::: stanza
‘I miss in his seat and in his bed
my friendly confidant[^42] — the sons of Gjúki are the cause\!
The sons of Gjúki are the cause of my suffering
and their sister’s sorrowful crying\!
:::

{#ch21-0022 ref="gdr1.21"}
::: stanza
‘Thus you[^43] will empty the land of its inhabitants,
as you did swear oaths;[^44]
you won’t, Gunnarr, profit from the gold;
those arm-rings will bring about your death,
since you[^45] swore oaths to Sigurðr.
:::

{#ch21-0023 ref="gdr1.22"}
::: stanza
‘There was often more merriment in the home-meadow,
when my Sigurðr saddled Grani,
and they went to ask for Brynhildr,
\[that\] wretched creature, with bad luck!’
:::

{#ch21-0024 ref="gdr1.23"}
::: stanza
Then Brynhildr said this, Buðli’s daughter:
‘May that creature be in want of husband and children
who brought you to tears, Guðrún,
and gave you speech-runes[^46] this morning!’
:::

{#ch21-0025 ref="gdr1.24"}
::: stanza
Then Gullrǫnd said this, Gjúki’s daughter:
‘Be silent, widely loathed one — \[enough\] of those words\!
You’ve always been the undoing[^47] of noblemen\!
Every evil fate of men drives you on —
\[you’re\] the sore sorrow of seven kings[^48]
and the worst[^49] friend-ruiner of women!’[^50]
:::

{#ch21-0026 ref="gdr1.25"}
::: stanza
Then Brynhildr said this, Buðli’s daughter:
‘Atli alone is the cause of all \[this\] evil,
\[the man\] born of Buðli, my brother\![^51]
:::

{#ch21-0027 ref="gdr1.26"}
::: stanza
‘When we two \[were\] in the hall of the Hunnish people,
we saw fire of the snake-bed[^52] on the boar;[^53]
I’ve since paid for \[that\] expedition,[^54]
for that sight — I was always uneasy about it!’
:::

{#ch21-0028 ref="gdr1.27"}
::: stanza
She stood by a post, she summoned her strength;
fire burnt from the eyes of Brynhildr,
Buðli’s daughter, \[and\] she snorted poison,
when she looked at the wounds on Sigurðr.
:::

{#ch21-0029}
Guðrún went away from there to a wood in a wasteland and went all the way to Danmǫrk [^55] and there stayed with Þóra, daughter of Hákon, for seven seasons.[^56]

{#ch21-0030}
Brynhildr did not want to live after Sigurðr. She had her eight slaves killed and five serving-women. Then she stabbed herself to death with a sword, as it says in {_Sigurðarkviða in skamma_} \[‘The Short Lay of Sigurðr’\].[^57]

[^6]: I.e., it was long ago.

[^7]: Or ‘… was about to die’. Cf. {_Gðr. II_} 11.

[^8]: The action of {_sitja yfir_} ‘sitting over’ (i.e., presumably, sitting beside and leaning over) a dead relative was probably an old Germanic custom.

[^9]: I.e., strike her hands together, an old Germanic gesture of grief. Cf. {_Gðr. II_} 11, {_Sg._} 25.

[^10]: Cf\. {_Gðr. II_} 11.

[^11]: They might include Gunnarr, whom Guðrún addresses directly in st. 21.

[^12]: Possibly three of the ‘earls’ of the preceding stanza.

[^13]: I.e., her own, not Guðrún’s.

[^14]: {_Gjaf-_} probably means ‘gift’, but the sense of {_-laug_} is uncertain.

[^15]: ‘Army Stronghold’.

[^16]: Land of the Huns.

[^17]: The side of their ship.

[^18]: Literally, ‘make them noble’, presumably by adorning their dead bodies.

[^19]: What preparation this involved is unknown, but perhaps it entailed the provision of goods for the journey to Hel.

[^20]: I.e., one half-year.

[^21]: Or ‘dress (in finery)’.

[^22]: ‘Gold-Edge/Shield’.

[^23]: Herborg\.

[^24]: Or cushion.

[^25]: For {_dǫglingr_} as ‘descendant of Dagr \[‘Day’\]’, see {_HH. I_} 7; alternatively, ‘the prince’s’.

[^26]: I.e., grown dim.

[^27]: Sigurðr’s.

[^28]: Or ‘courage-fortress’, i.e., his chest.

[^29]: Both an instance of pathetic fallacy and a metaphor for one of Guðrún’s tears.

[^30]: The interpretation of {_tresk_}, here translated ‘tresses’, is disputed.

[^31]: Literally, ‘yelled’.

[^32]: Cf\. {_Sg._} 29.

[^33]: Gullrǫnd is an otherwise almost unknown character.

[^34]: I.e., that of Guðrún and Sigurðr.

[^35]: Guðrún speaks at last.

[^36]: Garlic\.

[^37]: I.e., a gemstone.

[^38]: I.e., a cord.

[^39]: ‘Army Leader’, an alias of Óðinn. His women are valkyries, the most famous of whom, Brynhildr, is watching and listening.

[^40]: The precise sense of this passage is unclear.

[^41]: Sigurðr.

[^42]: Literally, ‘my conversation-friend’.

[^43]: Pl., in reference to the sons of Gjúki, her brothers.

[^44]: I.e., to Sigurðr.

[^45]: Sg\.

[^46]: I.e., got Guðrún to speak, as if by magic. Cf. {_Sd._} 12.

[^47]: Literally, ‘weird’, ‘(evil) fate’; a nonce identification with the Norn called {_Urðr_} is conceivable.

[^48]: If the number is to interpreted literally, the seven may be Sigurðr, Sigurðr’s son, Guthormr (Gothormr/Guttormr), Gunnarr, Hǫgni and two other sons of Gjúki (Gernoz and Giselher); cf. {_Br._} 8.

[^49]: Literally, ‘greatest’.

[^50]: Or ‘of wives’. The accusation is ambiguous as to whether it charges Brynhildr with being the worst example of a woman who ruins friendships or of a person who ruins women’s friendships.

[^51]: Brynhildr may be trying to exonerate herself by claiming that it was Atli’s desire for the Burgundians’ gold that gave rise to all the trouble.

[^52]: A kenning for ‘gold’, here probably that which Sigurðr took from Fáfnir’s lair.

[^53]: Presumably Sigurðr.

[^54]: Probably Sigurðr’s visit to the Hunnish hall.

[^55]: Denmark\.

[^56]: I.e., half-years. Cf. {_Gðr. II_} 14.

[^57]: This specific detail is not recorded in the following poem, which is entitled {_Kviða Sigurðar_} ‘Poem of Sigurðr’ in {*R*}, but which has come to be known as {_Sigurðarkviða in skamma_}. However, Brynhildr does stab herself fatally with a sword in chapter 9 of {_Norna-Gests þáttr_}.

